// automations.memql
//
// The work spine's two sweeps (design record
// docs/superpowers/specs/2026-09-05-work-spine-design.md, sections D
// "Waits" and "Retention", and E "Execute").
//
// Both are SCHEDULED, which is what gates them on the cron leader: a
// scheduled automation runs on one replica cluster-wide via the Postgres
// advisory lock in component/automations/cron_leader.go, so neither needs
// a lock of its own.
//
// BOTH RUN UNDER THE MAINTENANCE PRINCIPAL, and that is not optional.
// Every work concept declares @rowAuthz(owner="ownerUserId",
// clusterOwner). Under the default RoleReader system actor the owned
// branch matches nothing and the cluster-owner escape does not apply, so
// these reads would answer ZERO ROWS AND NO ERROR -- a sweep that resumes
// nothing looks exactly like a cluster with nothing parked. The entries
// are in component/auth/maintenance_actor.go, which is compiled in rather
// than annotated so a mounted product bundle cannot grant itself one.

/// The arguments are spelled here rather than defaulted in the handler: an empty call to a builtin
/// that declares fields is refused at load (memql#4927), and spelling them makes the schedule's
/// choice readable beside the schedule. 60s is twice the 30s heartbeat window, so a run is abandoned
/// only after two missed beats.
/// Every two minutes: resume runs whose timer wait is due, and re-claim runs whose node died
/// mid-flight. A timer wait carries resumeAt and no process is held open for it; an abandoned run
/// is one whose heartbeat stopped, and it is resumed elsewhere FROM THE JOURNAL rather than
/// restarted, so completed steps are served and never re-run.
@trigger(schedule="0 */2 * * * *")
automation sweepWaitingWorkRuns {
  sweep := builtin workSweepWaiting(olderThanSeconds: 60)
}

/// Nightly 03:40 UTC: archive then delete journal rows past their retention window. The run's
/// summary is folded onto the run row FIRST, so a run stays readable after its detail is gone, and
/// no archive means no delete -- the log store's rule, reused.
@trigger(schedule="0 40 3 * * *")
automation workJournalRetentionSweep {
  sweep := builtin workRetentionSweep(dryRun: false)
}

/// Check a goal run's answer the moment the run succeeds, before a person looks (epic memql#5414,
/// design D22). Fires on the TRANSITION only -- `args.oldStatus` is what makes a heartbeat or a later
/// summary write on an already-succeeded run not fire it again -- and only for a goal's run. Every
/// other rule is the handler's, which serves the check on agent nodes only (every other node skips
/// before it reads anything), reads the cluster's feedbackPolicy, skips a run that reached no
/// model, was not asked for through Nexus or the API, or is a driver-owned journal (an app
/// session's recording, the D7 delegate's child run) of another run's step, and never checks the
/// same answer version twice. One bounded model call at most, and it writes a decision observation and the run's
/// validation summary -- never a like, never a construct. The OWNER travels with the event, exactly
/// as learnFromSucceededRun's does: the handler borrows that one owner's authority for this trusted
/// completion trigger and re-reads the run under it, so a hint that does not own the run reads
/// nothing.
@trigger(event="node.updated", concept="v1:work:run")
@filter(row => row.status == "succeeded" && args.oldStatus != "succeeded" && row.goalId != "" && row.ownerUserId != "")
automation validateGoalAnswer {
  args {
    id           any
    oldStatus    any
    ownerUserId  any
  }
  check := builtin workValidateAnswer(runId: args.id ?? "", ownerUserId: args.ownerUserId ?? "")
}
